        "---\n",
        "title: \"N-Gram Language Models\"\n",
        "subtitle: \"Markov assumptions, conditional probability tables, smoothing, generation, and perplexity\"\n",
        "---\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.chapter-opening}\n",
        "An $n$-gram language model is one of the simplest mathematical models of language.  It predicts the next token using only the previous $n-1$ tokens.  Although modern transformer language models are much more powerful, $n$-gram models remain an essential starting point because their assumptions, estimators, failures, and evaluation metrics can be written down exactly.  In this chapter we study $n$-gram models as finite-state conditional probability models estimated from counts.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Learning goals\n",
        "\n",
        "After studying this chapter, you should be able to:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. define unigrams, bigrams, trigrams, and general $n$-grams;\n",
        "2. explain the Markov approximation behind an $n$-gram language model;\n",
        "3. estimate $n$-gram probabilities by maximum likelihood from corpus counts;\n",
        "4. represent an $n$-gram model as a conditional probability table over contexts;\n",
        "5. explain why zero counts create zero probabilities for unseen sentences;\n",
        "6. derive and implement add-$\\alpha$ smoothing;\n",
        "7. compute sentence log-probability, average negative log-likelihood, cross-entropy, and perplexity;\n",
        "8. sample text from a fitted $n$-gram model;\n",
        "9. compare unigram, bigram, and trigram models mathematically and computationally;\n",
        "10. use AI tools to critique a language model's assumptions without outsourcing the reasoning.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Why study $n$-gram models?\n",
        "\n",
        "An $n$-gram model is not a modern state-of-the-art NLP model.  However, it is mathematically transparent.  Nearly every important idea in language modeling appears in a simple form:\n",
        "\n",
        "- language as a probability distribution on token sequences;\n",
        "- conditioning on previous context;\n",
        "- maximum likelihood estimation;\n",
        "- sparse data and overfitting;\n",
        "- smoothing and regularization;\n",
        "- evaluation by cross-entropy and perplexity;\n",
        "- generation by sequential sampling.\n",
        "\n",
        "Modern neural language models replace explicit count tables with high-dimensional nonlinear functions, but they still model conditional probabilities of the form\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p_\\theta(w_t \\mid w_1,\\ldots,w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The $n$-gram model is the finite-table version of the same idea.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Central idea.**  An $n$-gram model approximates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_t=w_t \\mid W_1=w_1,\\ldots,W_{t-1}=w_{t-1})\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "by conditioning only on the most recent $n-1$ tokens:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_t=w_t \\mid W_{t-n+1}=w_{t-n+1},\\ldots,W_{t-1}=w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a mathematical simplification: the entire past is compressed into a short context.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Tokens, contexts, and $n$-grams\n",
        "\n",
        "Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "V=\\{v_1,v_2,\\ldots,v_d\\}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be a finite vocabulary.  A token sequence is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "w_{1:T}=(w_1,w_2,\\ldots,w_T), \\qquad w_t\\in V.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "An $n$-gram is a consecutive block of $n$ tokens:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "(w_t,w_{t+1},\\ldots,w_{t+n-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Common cases are:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Model | Unit | Example from `language models learn probability` |\n",
        "|---|---|---|\n",
        "| unigram | one token | `language`, `models`, `learn`, `probability` |\n",
        "| bigram | two tokens | `language models`, `models learn`, `learn probability` |\n",
        "| trigram | three tokens | `language models learn`, `models learn probability` |\n",
        "\n",
        "For prediction, it is useful to split an $n$-gram into a context and a next token.  For an $n$-gram model, the context length is $n-1$:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h_t=(w_{t-n+1},\\ldots,w_{t-1}), \\qquad \\text{next token } w_t.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The model estimates\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_t\\mid h_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a bigram model, the context is one token.  For a trigram model, the context is two tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The chain rule and the Markov approximation\n",
        "\n",
        "From Chapter 4, the chain rule gives the exact factorization\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_{1:T}=w_{1:T})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\prod_{t=1}^{T}P(W_t=w_t\\mid W_1=w_1,\\ldots,W_{t-1}=w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula is exact but not directly useful for estimation when $T$ is large.  The number of possible histories grows explosively.  The $n$-gram approximation assumes that the conditional distribution depends only on the most recent $n-1$ tokens:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_t=w_t\\mid W_1=w_1,\\ldots,W_{t-1}=w_{t-1})\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "P(W_t=w_t\\mid W_{t-n+1}=w_{t-n+1},\\ldots,W_{t-1}=w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(w_{1:T})\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "\\prod_{t=1}^{T}P(w_t\\mid w_{t-n+1:t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Here $w_{a:b}$ denotes the subsequence $(w_a,w_{a+1},\\ldots,w_b)$.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Markov viewpoint.**  A bigram model is a first-order Markov chain on the vocabulary:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W_t=w_t\\mid W_1,\\ldots,W_{t-1})\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "P(W_t=w_t\\mid W_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A trigram model is a second-order Markov model because it conditions on the previous two tokens.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This does not mean language truly has such short memory.  It means we choose a tractable approximation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Boundary tokens\n",
        "\n",
        "A practical language model needs to represent the beginning and end of a sentence.  We usually add special tokens such as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\langle s\\rangle, \\qquad \\langle/ s\\rangle.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For a bigram model, the sentence\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "language models learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "is transformed into\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "<s> language models learn </s>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "and contributes the bigrams\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "(<s>, language), (language, models), (models, learn), (learn, </s>)\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "For a trigram model, we often add two start tokens:\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "<s> <s> language models learn </s>\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "so that the first real word can be predicted from a two-token context.\n",
        "\n",
        "Mathematically, boundary tokens allow every position to have a context of the required length.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Maximum likelihood estimation for bigrams\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $C(u,v)$ denote the number of times token $u$ is followed by token $v$ in the training corpus.  Let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C(u)=\\sum_{v\\in V} C(u,v)\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "be the number of times $u$ appears as a context token.  The maximum likelihood estimator for a bigram model is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p(v\\mid u)=\\frac{C(u,v)}{C(u)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is simply a row-normalized count table.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Derivation\n",
        "\n",
        "For a fixed context $u$, suppose we observe counts\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C(u,v_1),C(u,v_2),\\ldots,C(u,v_d).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We want to estimate a conditional probability vector\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(\\cdot\\mid u)=(p(v_1\\mid u),\\ldots,p(v_d\\mid u))\\in \\Delta^{d-1}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The conditional likelihood for this row is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "L_u(p)=\\prod_{j=1}^{d} p(v_j\\mid u)^{C(u,v_j)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\ell_u(p)=\\sum_{j=1}^{d} C(u,v_j)\\log p(v_j\\mid u).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "We maximize $\\ell_u(p)$ subject to\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^{d}p(v_j\\mid u)=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Using a Lagrange multiplier $\\lambda$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\mathcal{L}(p,\\lambda)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^{d}C(u,v_j)\\log p(v_j\\mid u)\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\lambda\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^{d}p(v_j\\mid u)-1\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Differentiating with respect to $p(v_j\\mid u)$ gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{C(u,v_j)}{p(v_j\\mid u)}+\\lambda=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(v_j\\mid u)=-\\frac{C(u,v_j)}{\\lambda}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Summing over $j$ and using the constraint gives\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "1=\\sum_{j=1}^{d}p(v_j\\mid u)\n",
        "=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\sum_{j=1}^{d}C(u,v_j)\n",
        "=-\\frac{C(u)}{\\lambda}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "So $\\lambda=-C(u)$ and therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p(v_j\\mid u)=\\frac{C(u,v_j)}{C(u)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This derivation is important because it shows that the count ratio is not merely intuitive.  It is the maximum likelihood estimate under a multinomial model.\n",
        "\n",
        "## General $n$-gram maximum likelihood estimator\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $h$ be a context of length $n-1$.  For example, in a trigram model,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "h=(u_1,u_2).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Let $C(h,v)$ be the number of times context $h$ is followed by token $v$, and let\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C(h)=\\sum_{v\\in V}C(h,v).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The maximum likelihood estimator is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p(v\\mid h)=\\frac{C(h,v)}{C(h)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The same Lagrange multiplier argument applies independently to each context row.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Conditional probability table.**  An $n$-gram model is a collection of probability vectors\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\{p(\\cdot\\mid h): h\\in V^{n-1}\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Each row belongs to the probability simplex $\\Delta^{d-1}$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "For bigrams, this table is a $d\\times d$ matrix.  For trigrams, it is a $d^2\\times d$ table.  For general $n$, the number of possible contexts is $d^{n-1}$, which grows quickly.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Python example: building an $n$-gram model from scratch\n",
        "\n",
        "The following code builds an $n$-gram count model, estimates probabilities, and displays the next-token distribution for a chosen context."
      ],
