        "The model has learned, from this tiny corpus, which words tend to follow `models`.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The zero-probability problem\n",
        "\n",
        "Maximum likelihood $n$-gram models assign probability zero to any next-token event not observed in the training corpus:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C(h,v)=0\n",
        "\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n",
        "\\widehat p(v\\mid h)=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a serious issue.  If a single bigram in a test sentence has probability zero, then the entire sentence probability becomes zero:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\prod_{t=1}^{T}\\widehat p(w_t\\mid h_t)=0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The log-probability becomes undefined because $\\log 0=-\\infty$.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose the training corpus contains\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "language models learn\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "but never contains\n",
        "\n",
        "```text\n",
        "language models explain\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "A bigram model may estimate\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p(\\text{explain}\\mid \\text{models})=0,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "even though the phrase is perfectly reasonable.  The zero probability reflects limited data, not linguistic impossibility.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Add-$\\alpha$ smoothing\n",
        "\n",
        "A simple solution is add-$\\alpha$ smoothing.  For $\\alpha>0$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p_\\alpha(v\\mid h)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{C(h,v)+\\alpha}{C(h)+\\alpha |V|}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The numerator adds $\\alpha$ pseudo-counts to every possible next token.  The denominator adds the total pseudo-count mass $\\alpha |V|$.\n",
        "\n",
        "When $\\alpha=1$, this is called add-one or Laplace smoothing.  Smaller values such as $\\alpha=0.1$ often behave better in language modeling because the vocabulary may be large.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Smoothing as regularization.**  Add-$\\alpha$ smoothing moves each empirical row toward the uniform distribution:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\widehat p_\\alpha(\\cdot\\mid h)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{C(h)}{C(h)+\\alpha |V|}\\widehat p_{\\text{MLE}}(\\cdot\\mid h)\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\frac{\\alpha |V|}{C(h)+\\alpha |V|}\\,u,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where $u(v)=1/|V|$.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This formula shows that smoothing is a convex combination of the empirical MLE distribution and the uniform distribution.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python example: add-$\\alpha$ probabilities"
      ],
      "id": "5cc78648"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def next_token_distribution(context, vocab, context_counts, next_counts, alpha=0.5):\n",
        "    d = len(vocab)\n",
        "    total = context_counts[context]\n",
        "    return {\n",
        "        word: (next_counts[context][word] + alpha) / (total + alpha * d)\n",
        "        for word in vocab\n",
        "        if word != \"<s>\"\n",
        "    }\n",
        "\n",
        "context = (\"models\",)\n",
        "for alpha in [0.0, 0.1, 1.0]:\n",
        "    print(f\"\\nalpha = {alpha}\")\n",
        "    if alpha == 0.0:\n",
        "        # MLE: show only observed words to avoid many zeros\n",
        "        dist = {\n",
        "            word: next_counts[context][word] / context_counts[context]\n",
        "            for word in vocab\n",
        "            if context_counts[context] > 0 and next_counts[context][word] > 0\n",
        "        }\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        dist = next_token_distribution(context, vocab, context_counts, next_counts, alpha)\n",
        "    for word, prob in sorted(dist.items(), key=lambda x: -x[1])[:8]:\n",
        "        print(f\"P({word:16s} | models) = {prob:.3f}\")"
      ],
