        "The sentence probability is a product of local next-token probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Cross-entropy and perplexity\n",
        "\n",
        "Let a test corpus contain $N$ predicted tokens.  Suppose the model assigns probability $p_i$ to the correct token at position $i$.  The average negative log-likelihood is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{NLL}_{\\text{avg}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\frac{1}{N}\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\log p_i.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "When natural logarithms are used, this quantity is measured in nats per token.  The perplexity is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Perplexity}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\exp\\left(\\operatorname{NLL}_{\\text{avg}}\\right).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Equivalently,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{Perplexity}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\left(\\prod_{i=1}^{N}\\frac{1}{p_i}\\right)^{1/N}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "So perplexity is the geometric mean of the inverse probabilities assigned to the correct next tokens.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Interpretation.**  Lower perplexity is better.  A perplexity of $10$ roughly means that, on average, the model is as uncertain as choosing uniformly among $10$ plausible next tokens.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "This interpretation is approximate, but it is useful for intuition.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Python example: evaluating perplexity"
      ],
      "id": "422dc224"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "def evaluate_perplexity(test_corpus, n, vocab, context_counts, next_counts, alpha=0.5):\n",
        "    total_logp = 0.0\n",
        "    total_tokens = 0\n",
        "\n",
        "    for sentence in test_corpus:\n",
        "        tokens = add_boundaries(tokenize(sentence), n)\n",
        "        d = len(vocab)\n",
