        "The generated text may be grammatical in small fragments, but it often lacks long-range coherence.  This is a mathematical consequence of the short-context assumption.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Comparing unigram, bigram, and trigram models\n",
        "\n",
        "A unigram model assumes tokens are independent:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_{1:T})\\approx \\prod_{t=1}^{T}p(w_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A bigram model keeps one previous token:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_{1:T})\\approx \\prod_{t=1}^{T}p(w_t\\mid w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "A trigram model keeps two previous tokens:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_{1:T})\\approx \\prod_{t=1}^{T}p(w_t\\mid w_{t-2},w_{t-1}).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "As $n$ increases, the model can represent richer local structure.  But the number of possible contexts grows as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "|V|^{n-1}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This creates a bias-variance tradeoff.\n",
        "\n",
        "| Model | Context length | Strength | Weakness |\n",
        "|---|---:|---|---|\n",
        "| unigram | 0 | stable estimates | ignores order |\n",
        "| bigram | 1 | captures local transitions | limited memory |\n",
        "| trigram | 2 | captures short phrases | sparse counts |\n",
        "| larger $n$ | $n-1$ | more context | severe sparsity |\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.math-card}\n",
        "**Bias-variance viewpoint.**  Small $n$ gives high bias because the model is too simple.  Large $n$ can give high variance because many contexts have few or no observations.  Smoothing partially controls variance but cannot fully solve the curse of dimensionality.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Backoff and interpolation\n",
        "\n",
        "Add-$\\alpha$ smoothing is simple but not always strong enough.  Two common ideas are backoff and interpolation.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Backoff\n",
        "\n",
        "If a trigram context is rare or unseen, use a lower-order model:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_t\\mid w_{t-2},w_{t-1})\n",
        "\\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad\n",
        "p(w_t\\mid w_{t-1})\n",
        "\\quad \\longrightarrow \\quad\n",
        "p(w_t).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The model backs off from specific context to more general context.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Interpolation\n",
        "\n",
        "Interpolation combines several orders:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "p(w_t\\mid w_{t-2},w_{t-1})\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\lambda_3 p_3(w_t\\mid w_{t-2},w_{t-1})\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\lambda_2 p_2(w_t\\mid w_{t-1})\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\lambda_1 p_1(w_t),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\lambda_1,\\lambda_2,\\lambda_3\\geq 0,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2+\\lambda_3=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This is a convex combination of probability distributions.  It is mathematically similar to ensemble methods and shrinkage estimators.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Matrix viewpoint for bigram models\n",
        "\n",
        "For a vocabulary of size $d$, a bigram model can be represented by a transition matrix\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P\\in R^{d\\times d},\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "where\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_{ij}=P(W_t=v_j\\mid W_{t-1}=v_i).\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Each row is a probability vector:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P_{ij}\\geq 0,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "\\sum_{j=1}^{d}P_{ij}=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus $P$ is a row-stochastic matrix.  A distribution $\\pi_t$ over the current token evolves by\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\pi_{t+1}=\\pi_t P.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "This connects bigram language modeling to finite Markov chains.\n",
        "\n",
